DPE-MVS performs the best on the benchmark and it ranks first on #B.first_count.at(k.dpe) of the #B.n_scenes scenes with a mean F1-score of #fix(_f(k.dpe).mean, 3) at 2~cm. CUMVS(APD) \@ native and APD-MVS are next with #fix(_f(k.oursnat).mean, 3) and #fix(_f(k.apd).mean, 3), and are compared in #ref(<sec:bench-cumvs-apd>). It is important to note that these three methods run at native resolution giving them an unfair advantage due to having access to four times as much pixel information. The best quality capped method is #lab(B.best_capped), with #fix(_f(B.best_capped).mean, 3) F1-score at 2~cm. ACMH, the initial algorithm in the lineage, is last with an F1-score of #fix(_f(k.acmh).mean, 3) (#ref(<table-bench-main>)). This is expected due to not having all the compute heavy completeness machinery that other algorithms introduced. ACMH is the best performing in accuracy, likely because it only keeps candidates that it truly trusts through strong photometric constraints, meanwhile lacking concepts like planar priors that would let it estimate points on textureless surfaces at a much lower accuracy. On the #spell(B.common_scenes.len()) scenes that HPM-MVS++ completed, its F1 of #fix(AC.at(k.hpmpp).f1_2cm.mean, 3) lies between #lab(B.hpmpp_common_above.last()) (#fix(AC.at(B.hpmpp_common_above.last()).f1_2cm.mean, 3)) and #lab(B.hpmpp_common_below_next) (#fix(AC.at(B.hpmpp_common_below_next).f1_2cm.mean, 3)). Therefore, HPM-MVS++ is likely an equivalent or better performing capped candidate than MP-MVS, had it finished all of its scenes (see the detailed results in #ref(<app-bench>)). // src: v.rank_2cm; v.arms.*.f1_2cm; v.arms_common.*.f1_2cm, v.hpmpp_common_above, v.hpmpp_common_below_next
